project structure explanation
Overview
I built a command-line equipment lending program in Python using only the standard library. The program's state is held in three variables: resources (the inventory), fellows (a dictionary of fellow IDs to names) and borrow_records (the loan log). Each feature is a separate function, and a looping menu connects them to the user.
Functions
• add_resource() validates the fields, rejects duplicate IDs and non-positive totals, and adds the resource with available equal to total.
• borrow() checks the fellow ID, resource ID, that the quantity is a positive integer, and that it does not exceed available stock. Only then does it reduce available and log a loan record.
• return_item() calculates how many units the fellow currently holds and rejects any return larger than that. On success it updates both the loan records and the inventory.
• search_by_name() lowercases the search term and each resource name, so the search ignores case.
• generate_report() calculates total, available and borrowed units, lists resources with fewer than 3 available, and finds the most borrowed resource, including ties.
Helper functions include find_resource(), parse_positive_int(), outstanding(), filter_by_category(), save_data(), load_data() and menu().
How data is represented
Inventory is a list of dictionaries, each with id, name, category, total and available. Loans are stored as a list of dictionaries in borrow_records. Each successful borrowing creates one record with fellow_id, resource_id, quantity and returned. A fellow's current loan for a resource is the sum of quantity - returned across their records for that resource. Returns increase returned, starting with the oldest record. The system stays consistent because total = available + units currently borrowed.
Design decisions
Logic functions return (ok, message) and do not read input or print, so the menu handles all user interaction. Every function validates first and changes state last, so rejected attempts never alter the data.
Limitation
available is stored separately from the loan records, so the same fact exists in two places. If the saved JSON file were edited by hand, or a bug updated only one of them, they could disagree and the reports would be wrong. The program also has no due dates, cannot add fellows from the menu, and holds everything in memory, so it is not safe for several users at once.
Why return_item checks the fellow's loan, not the stock
The rule is about what that person holds, not what is on the shelf. John borrowed 3 keyboards, so he can only return up to 3, even though the shelf could hold more. If the check used stock, someone could "return" items they never borrowed and inflate the inventory.


